    <h2>It's Almost Always the Spring, Not the Opener</h2>

    <p>A garage door does not weigh what it feels like when the opener lifts it. A standard two-car door runs well over 150 pounds, and the reason a small motor can raise it is the torsion spring mounted above the opening, wound tight to counterbalance nearly all that weight. The opener is a nudge, not a lift. When a spring breaks — and they break, because they are rated for a finite number of cycles, roughly 10,000, which is about 7 to 14 years of normal use — the counterbalance is gone and the opener is suddenly asked to hoist the full dead weight it was never sized for. So it hums, strains, and quits. The homeowner hears a dying motor. What they are hearing is a healthy motor defeated by a broken spring.</p>

    <h2>Why "Buy a New Opener" Fails</h2>

    <p>Because the opener is what has the buttons and the motor and the noise, it gets the blame, and the reflex is to replace it. The mistake plays out like this: the homeowner spends $350 to $600 on a new opener, the installer bolts it up, and it hums and quits exactly like the old one — because the actual failure, the snapped spring, is still sitting there. Now they have paid for an opener they never needed and still have to pay for the spring. The other version of this mistake is more dangerous: pulling the red emergency release with a broken spring lets the unbalanced door slam down under its own weight, which is how fingers and hoods get hurt.</p>

    <h2>The Counterintuitive Part: The Cheap Fix Is the Right Fix</h2>

    <p>It runs against instinct, but the inexpensive repair is usually the correct one here. A spring replacement is a couple hundred dollars and a same-day job; the expensive opener replacement is the one people talk themselves into. The honest admission a good technician makes: your opener is probably fine, and I could sell you one you don't need, but the spring is your problem. A tech who leads with replacing the whole system before checking the spring is either not looking or not honest. The tell is simple and visible — walk into the garage, look at the spring bar above the door, and a broken spring shows a clear 2-inch gap where the coil separated.</p>

    <h2>What Actually Works — and the One Job to Leave Alone</h2>

    <p>Real garage door service is diagnosis first: check the spring, the cables, the rollers, and the track before condemning the opener. Springs get replaced in pairs, because when one of a matched set fails after the same number of cycles, the other is not far behind, and replacing both saves you a second service call within the year. The one hard line, and it is a safety line: do not DIY a torsion spring. Under full tension a spring holds enough stored energy to whip a winding bar back and break an arm, and the emergency rooms see it every year. This is the opposite of a job like swapping a light fixture that a capable <a href="/locations/hartselle/handyman/">handyman</a> handles fine — spring work needs the bars, the clamps, and someone who has done it a hundred times. If the opener itself does turn out to be the failure, or the wall wiring is suspect, that is where a <a href="/locations/hartselle/electrician/">licensed electrician</a> comes in.</p>

