    <p>New subdivisions like Turtle Pond Estates and the homes out toward Priceville came with builder-grade doors and openers, and those are the ones now hitting the age where a first spring lets go — a repair, not a reason to replace the whole door.</p>

    <h2>When It's a Today Problem</h2>

    <p>A broken spring is a today problem — the car is trapped or the house is standing open — and most Hartselle companies run same-day spring service for exactly that reason. If your door still works but is getting loud, jerky, or slow, that is worn rollers and hinges talking, and servicing them before a cable frays is cheaper than the failure it prevents. If a spring has already broken once and the door is a decade-plus old, ask about replacing both springs and the worn cables together rather than one part at a time.</p>

    <h2>Garage Door Questions Worth Asking</h2>

    <p><strong>Opener hums but the door won't move — new opener?</strong></p>
    <p>Usually not. It's almost always a snapped torsion spring; the opener can't lift the door's full weight without it. Look for a 2-inch gap in the spring coil.</p>

    <p><strong>Can I change the spring myself?</strong></p>
    <p>No. Torsion springs hold enough tension to seriously injure you. This is the one garage job that genuinely requires a pro.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Spring replacement $150 to $350 same day, a new opener $350 to $600, a full door $900 to $2,500.</p>

